      id: "what-paytabs",
      heading: "What PayTabs is in your stack, and which UAE merchant it genuinely fits",
      paragraphs: [
        `PayTabs is a payment gateway with operations across several Middle East and North African markets, including the UAE. In your architecture it occupies the gateway position: it collects the card details, moves the transaction through the card networks, and reports the outcome back to your server. It is not your bank, it is not your accountant, and it is not the party that decides whether a disputed charge sticks.`,
        `The one structural fact worth understanding before anything else is that a PayTabs merchant account is organised as a profile that belongs to a market. The UAE profile is a distinct object from a profile in another country, with its own credentials, its own settlement arrangement and its own enabled method list. Almost every confusing thing that happens later in a PayTabs integration traces back to that single fact, which is why it gets its own section further down rather than a footnote here.`,
        `The merchant this suits best is a UAE business that already knows, or strongly suspects, that it will sell in more than one Gulf market, and a business whose sales are not purely cart-shaped. If half your revenue arrives as an invoice to a corporate buyer and half as a web checkout, a provider whose toolkit treats payment links as a first-class way to get paid saves you building that yourself. It also suits a merchant who wants the card field rendered by the provider but inside their own page rather than on a redirect.`,
        `One caveat applies to every sentence on this page. Gateway field names, endpoints, method lists, dashboard layouts and plugin availability all change, sometimes between the day you read a tutorial and the day you write the code. What follows is the shape of a PayTabs integration and the decisions it forces on a UAE merchant, written to still be useful when a parameter has been renamed. Build from PayTabs' current developer documentation, and get anything that would cost you money confirmed in writing by PayTabs rather than inferred from a blog post.`,
      ],
